1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,561
Open the C09 template.

2
00:00:01,561 --> 00:00:06,131
One row per agent: the task it replaces, the time it used to take you, the time you

3
00:00:06,131 --> 00:00:10,701
spend checking it now, and the AI cost straight from your own provider's dashboard,

4
00:00:10,701 --> 00:00:12,683
not a guess you made up on the spot.

5
00:00:12,683 --> 00:00:16,978
You already own most of the raw material, which is why this lands here and not

6
00:00:16,978 --> 00:00:17,418
earlier.

7
00:00:17,418 --> 00:00:21,988
The trial tallies from your sticky notes, the flags agreed with, the drafts sent as

8
00:00:21,988 --> 00:00:23,695
written, are the evidence side.

9
00:00:23,695 --> 00:00:28,265
What C09 adds is the time dimension, and a cadence: fill it in weekly for the first

10
00:00:28,265 --> 00:00:32,615
six weeks, while the numbers are still moving, then quarterly once they settle.

11
00:00:32,615 --> 00:00:37,130
Priya's own six week log is where the next lecture's total comes from, so what you

12
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:39,332
are building here is not an admin chore.

13
00:00:39,332 --> 00:00:42,691
It is the exact document that turns "trust me" into a number.

14
00:00:43,241 --> 00:00:47,585
This single habit is what separates a number you can defend in a meeting from one

15
00:00:47,585 --> 00:00:50,803
that gets quietly challenged and undermines the whole pitch.

16
00:00:50,803 --> 00:00:54,450
Five minutes of honesty now saves you an uncomfortable moment later.

17
00:00:54,450 --> 00:00:58,955
How do you actually measure checking time without turning your week into a stopwatch

18
00:00:58,955 --> 00:00:59,438
exercise?

19
00:00:59,438 --> 00:01:03,782
One representative week is enough: for seven days, jot the minutes each morning's

20
00:01:03,782 --> 00:01:08,019
review actually took, then use that as the row's standing value until something

21
00:01:08,019 --> 00:01:08,448
changes.

22
00:01:08,448 --> 00:01:12,899
And watch for the quiet cheat that inflates every tracker eventually: once checking

23
00:01:12,899 --> 00:01:17,404
feels routine, people stop counting it, and the saving silently grows on paper while

24
00:01:17,404 --> 00:01:18,906
nothing changed at the desk.

25
00:01:18,906 --> 00:01:23,357
The checking time never goes to zero in this method, so it never leaves the tracker

26
00:01:23,357 --> 00:01:23,733
either.

27
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:27,916
If a future you finds a row where it has, that row is fiction, and Section 7's

28
00:01:27,916 --> 00:01:30,061
rubric has a name for what happens next.

29
00:01:30,611 --> 00:01:34,956
That sentence works because it names the saving, the cost, and the safeguard in one

30
00:01:34,956 --> 00:01:35,322
breath.

31
00:01:35,322 --> 00:01:39,196
A number with no safeguard mentioned sounds reckless to a careful manager.

32
00:01:39,196 --> 00:01:43,331
A safeguard with no number sounds like you have not actually measured anything.

33
00:01:43,331 --> 00:01:47,727
Write your own version now, while the habit of counting honestly is still fresh from

34
00:01:47,727 --> 00:01:49,298
filling in the tracker itself.

35
00:01:49,298 --> 00:01:53,485
The two words doing the most work are "cleanly counted", because they answer the

36
00:01:53,485 --> 00:01:55,840
question a careful listener was about to ask.

37
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:00,133
Every manager has been shown a savings figure that evaporated under one follow-up,

38
00:02:00,133 --> 00:02:04,163
and the phrase tells them, before they ask, that the checking time is already

39
00:02:04,163 --> 00:02:06,937
subtracted and the soft benefits are not smuggled in.

40
00:02:06,937 --> 00:02:08,821
You are not just reporting a number.

41
00:02:08,821 --> 00:02:13,218
You are reporting which kind of person counted it, and that second signal is the one

42
00:02:13,218 --> 00:02:14,317
that gets remembered.

43
00:02:14,867 --> 00:02:19,702
Next lecture puts a real total against these four agents, and names the one task on

44
00:02:19,702 --> 00:02:23,081
Priya's list she decided, deliberately, never to automate.
